{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxsGh7ZzmbSXWyBhSFkA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've finished all four steps and checked each one by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' reads the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index, and its time field is `timestamp`. The HTTP response code is in `response`, stored as text, with an exact-match version in `response.keyword`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-00dd48a3 Web logs' (id `s4-00dd48a3-web-logs-c4c10ddaa8ca`) with exactly two panels:\n   - A line chart of the request count over `timestamp`.\n   - A bar chart of the request count for each value of `response.keyword`, showing the top 20 codes.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-00dd48a3 5xx errors' (id `1c339d3b-2909-40ae-a16b-5ff95fb6020f`). It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. Because the response code is stored as text rather than a number, I matched it with the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]` on `response.keyword`. That counts every code from 500 to 599 and nothing else. I created the rule already enabled.\n4. **Panel edit and rule check:** The response-code panel's title is now 'Responses by code', and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows it enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":285,"cache_read_input_tokens":21322,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":285},"output_tokens":497,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-58d9-b388-fca8d0eba393","uuid":"39698179-f07d-4964-9544-476643bbd3b5","timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:07:25.095Z","request_id":"req_011Cfxs